WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.536
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy- and Tony-nominated

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.204
and Academy Award-winning
actor and singer

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:06.973
you know from her work
in "West Side Story,"

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.675
"Schmigadoon!",
and "Hamilton" on Broadway.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.144
She stars in the new
animated movie "Wish,"

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.880
which is in theaters
nationwide on November 22nd.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.215
Please welcome to the show
Ariana DeBose, everybody.

00:00:16.215 --> 00:00:18.485
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:42.007
-Whoo!

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:45.445
Hey, y'all! Hi!

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:49.749
-When someone like you,
who knows how to dance,

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:51.551
the way you just walk
is so much --

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:52.585
it makes me so jealous.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.853
When someone like you walks out,

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:55.922
this is how I feel like
I walk out.

00:00:57.456 --> 00:00:59.692
-I mean, your timing,
though, is really good.

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.494
-Thank you very much.
-Amazing.

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:02.795
-I'm so happy
to have you here.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:04.163
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:06.332
-You have not been in this
building since you hosted

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:09.635
a wonderful episode of "SNL"
in January of '22, I believe?

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.604
-I think that's correct, yeah.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.639
-Was it a special time
to be here for that?

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:17.142
-It was.
I mean, why was I hosting "SNL"?

00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:19.579
[ Laughs ]

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:20.780
But it was so fun.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.948
And, I mean,
it's such an inherently

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:24.751
theatrical experience.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.919
-Yes.
-It's absolutely insane.

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:30.623
And I don't know why people say
yes to it, but it's so fun.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:35.194
And I it's funny,
because I feel a little nervy.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:36.929
Like, you can still feel the
nervous energy in the building.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:38.464
-I agree.
Like, sometimes for people

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.166
who don't, like, come here
every day, they're like,

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.635
"Oh, right, there was that very
scary thing down the hall."

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:44.436
-Trauma bond, trauma bond.

00:01:44.436 --> 00:01:47.973
But it's so -- I don't know.
It's -- I love the show

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:50.476
And I love this place.
It's iconic.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:53.146
So anytime you get to be
in the space is great.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.715
-I -- For me, hosting "SNL"
was one of the most

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:57.183
nerve-racking things I ever did.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:59.185
But I heard you -- And you did
such a wonderful job

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.419
hosting the Tonys.

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:02.055
-Oh, thank you.
-Congratulations.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:04.857
-Thank you. I appreciate that.
Yeah.

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:09.228
-And the timing of that
was also not ideal.

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.297
It was right after
the writers' strike.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:14.167
You very happily received
a waiver from the Guild

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:16.402
to go on with the show,
which was wonderful to support

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:17.570
all the incredible people
and the work they did that year.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.771
-Of course.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:21.874
-The opening
of the show was a dance number

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:24.777
that was just incredible
to behold.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:25.778
-Thank you.

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.713
-I mean,
the normal dancing stuff,

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:29.248
which you do better
than anyone,

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.317
but you also jumped off
the top of the stairs.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:35.054
-Yes, I did that for Broadway.
-Yes.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.757
-No, that whole number
was kind of --

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.893
I mean, of course it was born
of the moment we were in, right?

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:44.763
And, so, I play to my strengths
when I'm in a tight spot.

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:47.399
And I was like, "Cool, we're
going to do a dance overture

00:02:47.399 --> 00:02:49.134
and we're going
to celebrate music

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:51.204
and we're going
to celebrate movement."

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:56.442
And I pulled together a great
team, and we made it happen.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:03:00.146
And I have to say, though,

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:04.550
that jump off the stairs wasn't
really rehearsed that way.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.251
-Yeah. Oh, wow.
-No. Like --

00:03:06.251 --> 00:03:07.953
-So, who did it different? You?

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:09.822
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.157
-Like I -- You know --

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:13.092
-Because I should note
that someone caught you,

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.228
so I would hope --
That would be the only person

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:16.795
I would think
you'd want to rehearse with.

00:03:16.795 --> 00:03:18.530
-Oh, no, of course. But we did.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.233
So, Julius Anthony Rubio
is a dear, dear friend of mine,

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:23.436
and he was one of the dancers
in "West Side Story."

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.138
And so when I decided
I was going to

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.506
jump down the stairs,
I was like, "Cool, cool.

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:28.374
You gonna catch me."
So...

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:29.976
-Is he, like,
better at catching than most?

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.310
-Oh, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.380
-I mean, he is -- He's the guy
you want to catch you.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.917
And I had rehearsed
safety first, you know,

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:40.553
so you go up and back, right?

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:45.591
Simple. I had so much adrenaline
in my body, I just said, "Boom!"

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:46.926
-You really did.

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.028
-It was --
So, to be perfectly frank,

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.531
that moment is kind of
like TV magic,

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:53.599
because I wasn't
supposed to do that.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:55.434
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.603
-The crazy thing is,
I was watching and I thought,

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:58.771
"God, look at that.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.239
She's probably done
that a thousand times,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:02.241
and she makes it look
like the first time ever."

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:03.476
-Oh!

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:05.511
-And now I'm finding out,
oh, first time ever.

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.480
There you go.
-So fresh. It was so fresh.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.182
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:12.251
-I definitely --
With the writers on strike,

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:14.019
I watched that incredible
dance overture and I'm like,

00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:15.588
"Oh, we don't need
writers, yeah."

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.156
-Well -- No, no, no, no.

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:19.391
I love my writers.
Most everything I do --

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:21.093
-I'm joking. I'm joking.
-I know.

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:23.396
But I feel like there
are many writers

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.064
who would kill me
if I didn't say this.

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:27.900
And I am here because
of some incredible writers.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.568
Tony Kushner being one of them.
So shout-out, Tony Kushner.

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:30.669
-Yeah, there you go.
Shout-out to Tony Kushner.

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:31.804
-Yeah.
-One of the all time greats.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:33.172
-Yeah.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:35.908
-You've talked about your mom
many times in the past.

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:38.744
Does she -- I'm assuming
on an evening like the Tonys,

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:40.145
is she there?

00:04:40.145 --> 00:04:41.914
Is she supporting you?
Is she going into the parties?

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.716
-Well, you know,
this particular year,

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.084
this past year,
she was not there.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.753
But, normally, my mom
is my date for everything.

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:50.122
She came to the Oscars.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:53.225
She was my -- I think
my favorite memory of my mom

00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:55.928
is at what I call
the "Hamil-Tony Party."

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.262
So it was the year
that "Hamilton"

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:59.064
was honored with
many, many things.

00:04:59.064 --> 00:05:04.069
And she literally
was just having the best time.

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:07.339
I looked over, and she was
cutting a rug with Common.

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:09.175
-Oh, wow.
-Like, amazing.

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:12.478
I was like, "Mom,
did you just hit on Common?"

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:13.845
Like -- And then
Mariska Hargitay was,

00:05:13.845 --> 00:05:15.748
like, jammin' behind her,

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.249
and I was like,
"What is happening?"

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:20.018
But moms and famous people
are the best.

00:05:20.018 --> 00:05:25.792
-My mom danced with Idris Elba
once at an Emmy party,

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:29.028
and my dad still
can't watch "The Wire," yeah.

00:05:29.028 --> 00:05:31.564
It's too hard for him now.
It's too hard for him.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:32.598
-But come on.

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:34.167
I mean,
the entertainment industry

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.068
is filled with beautiful people,
and so sometimes,

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:38.404
I'm just like,
"Yeah, Mom, go get yours."

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:39.772
-And you know what? Can I say?

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:42.641
I want to give a tip of my cap
to the beautiful people

00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:44.477
who every now and then
appreciate the moment.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:45.845
They're like, "You know what?

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.847
I'm going to make someone's mom
feel good right now."

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:49.982
And so I have a very
special place in my heart

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.417
for Idris Elba for that moment.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:53.186
And I feel like Common,
as well, right?

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:57.423
-Absolutely. Shout-out to both.
-Shout-out to both of you.

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.825
I have a lot more to ask
about your exciting new film.

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:02.028
We'll be right back
with Ariana after this.

